WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.919
The tsunami has completely flooded the area.

00:00:02.919 --> 00:00:05.213
The entire area is flooded.

00:00:05.213 --> 00:00:06.589
Please lower your use.

00:00:06.589 --> 00:00:08.091
I can confirm.

00:00:08.091 --> 00:00:08.508
Roger that.

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:15.557
Ten years after the Fukushima disaster,

00:00:16.349 --> 00:00:18.226
scenes like these are still hard to watch.

00:00:22.313 --> 00:00:26.609
The 2011 earthquake and tsunami devastated northeastern Japan,

00:00:27.318 --> 00:00:29.112
killing more than 15,000,

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:32.323
leveling more than 120,000 homes,

00:00:33.616 --> 00:00:37.245
and costing upwards of $200 billion in damage.

00:00:38.246 --> 00:00:43.001
The force of the blast disagrees with the official line that the reactor itself survived.

00:00:43.626 --> 00:00:44.252
Inconceivable.

00:00:45.003 --> 00:00:45.754
Ten years later,

00:00:46.004 --> 00:00:49.299
the scars of the earthquake and nuclear disaster are still visible.

00:00:53.678 --> 00:00:53.970
Today,

00:00:54.637 --> 00:01:00.435
the population of Fukushima Prefecture remains 180,000 less than before the triple disaster.

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:09.152
Vice World News traveled back to Fukushima weeks before the 10th anniversary to meet some of the people who,

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:10.445
despite it all,

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:11.905
refused to leave.

00:01:26.044 --> 00:01:27.045
This is Namiya Town,

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:30.757
20 kilometers from the Daiichi nuclear power plants,

00:01:31.341 --> 00:01:35.261
the site of the second worst nuclear accident in human history.

00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:38.264
Before the disaster,

00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:41.559
some 21,000 people called Namiya Town home.

00:01:42.977 --> 00:01:43.269
Today,

00:01:43.812 --> 00:01:46.439
the population is little more than 1,200.

00:01:49.859 --> 00:01:51.653
We're on our way to meet Yoshizawa-san,

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:53.863
who owns a ranch here in Namiya Town.

00:01:54.489 --> 00:01:57.283
And he was one of the very first residents to return to the town,

00:01:57.492 --> 00:02:00.078
even though it was still declared unsafe.

00:02:00.662 --> 00:02:03.123
And we want to ask him what made him take that risk,

00:02:03.998 --> 00:02:04.833
why he returned,

00:02:04.916 --> 00:02:06.709
and what he thinks of Namiya Town

00:02:07.377 --> 00:02:08.753
10 years from the disaster.

00:02:21.099 --> 00:02:23.726
Masami Yoshizawa used to be a dairy farmer.

00:02:24.727 --> 00:02:28.231
Then radiation from the nearby nuclear plant sickened his herd.

00:02:29.232 --> 00:02:31.609
He has 257 cows remaining.

00:02:33.194 --> 00:02:36.656
All of them are too radioactive to consume their meat or milk.

00:02:49.794 --> 00:02:54.632
What happened to the cows after you had to evacuated?

00:02:55.216 --> 00:03:02.140
The deal I had to sell 330 cows was cancelled.
They told me, "don't bring them.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:07.729
They said, "[the cows] are radioactive."

00:03:07.729 --> 00:03:11.900
The boss who made the deal said it was over.
Everything was over. He told me to run.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:12.734
uSv/h

00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:18.156
Everyone within this 20 kilometer
perimeter had to evacuate immediately.

00:03:18.156 --> 00:03:21.576
So I had to escape while all my cows were still tethered here.

00:03:21.576 --> 00:03:26.998
Some dairy farmers left with their cows still tied up.
The animals eventually died of starvation.

00:03:27.498 --> 00:03:31.085
They had no water, no food. The cows became mummies.

00:03:31.753 --> 00:03:34.589
I stayed at the ranch for about a week, until March 17 or 18.

00:03:35.506 --> 00:03:45.016
I had 330 cows to take care of.
I couldn't just evacuate immediately.

00:03:50.438 --> 00:03:51.522
After that, the government told us to "dispose" of all
surviving livestock. That was the instructions we received.

00:03:51.773 --> 00:03:55.276
After that, the government told us to "dispose" of all

00:03:55.276 --> 00:03:56.861
After that, the government told us to "dispose" oall
surviving livestock. That was the instructions we received.

00:03:58.279 --> 00:04:06.204
They barricaded the area on April 22, 2011.
We couldn't get in. We couldn't take care of the cows.

00:04:06.204 --> 00:04:15.213
Most dairy farmers had no choice
but to follow the government's orders.

00:04:15.213 --> 00:04:21.970
All stray cows had to be captured and killed.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:28.393
So within this 20 kilometer barricade,
our only options were to starve or kill our cows.

00:04:29.394 --> 00:04:39.237
You can't go back to being a farmer,
once you've abandoned them like that.

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:42.573
I've been a dairy farmer for a long time,

00:04:42.657 --> 00:04:48.413
so I've always considered myself one with the animals. I continued
to take care of them. I didn't follow the government's orders.

00:05:05.263 --> 00:05:09.934
In one farm, mixed among all the bones,
we found one surviving cow.

00:05:11.102 --> 00:05:18.943
It didn't leave. We were asked to rescue it, so we took it in.

00:05:19.694 --> 00:05:24.782
It somehow survived while
all the others died or were killed.

00:05:24.782 --> 00:05:25.700
Is this cow still alive?

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:31.456
Yes. There was nothing to drink because of
the power outages, so I released them all from the barn.

00:05:33.374 --> 00:05:39.964
There were so many cows. They were
waiting for us to come back and give them food.

00:05:40.548 --> 00:05:42.300
"A space between 'the police' and 'break through'?"

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:49.891
shouldn't be there. Should be "We'd come every three days
to break the barrier with our truck, and deliver the cows' food."

00:05:50.475 --> 00:05:53.061
That's why they were able to survive.

00:05:53.728 --> 00:05:59.650
This is from one year later. Suddenly,
these spots began to appear on the cows.

00:05:59.817 --> 00:06:06.240
These white spots on the black Wagyu cows.

00:06:06.407 --> 00:06:10.078
They were eating radioactive grass.
One year later, they had these spots.

00:06:11.412 --> 00:06:22.256
Their hides and skin are both black.
But in these areas, it turned white.

00:06:23.174 --> 00:06:27.845
One year after the disaster, 20 cows suddenly had these spots.

00:06:27.929 --> 00:06:29.347
And these cows are still alive?

00:06:29.514 --> 00:06:30.098
Yes.

00:06:47.782 --> 00:06:54.372
When you treat life in this horrible way,
I think it leaves deep scars on your heart.

00:06:56.290 --> 00:07:00.128
It's like trauma. The wounds on your heart never go away.

00:07:00.294 --> 00:07:03.214
Using the compensation money from TEPCO,

00:07:03.381 --> 00:07:13.307
I continued to take care of the cows.
I'm not sure how long I can keep doing this though.

00:07:13.474 --> 00:07:17.395
I never thought about leaving this place.

00:07:17.562 --> 00:07:24.402
This is my hometown. This is
where I'll end my life, at this farm.

00:07:28.156 --> 00:07:31.242
This bone is from the spine of a dead cow.

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:38.749
These two connect like this. This is
where the stomach and intestines are.

00:07:42.003 --> 00:07:46.591
This is out of respect for the cows.
It's a marker for their graves.

00:07:46.591 --> 00:07:53.097
The character is called "obagyu." It's a cow
ghost. They died and came back as ghosts.

00:07:54.182 --> 00:07:59.270
Their souls are still floating around on this farm.

00:08:01.022 --> 00:08:05.401
The soil surrounding Yoshizawa's ranch is still contaminated by radiation,

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:10.072
with some locations measuring above the international safety threshold.

00:08:11.782 --> 00:08:15.703
1.4. About one meter from the ground,
the radioactivity is 1.4 microsieverts.

00:08:15.703 --> 00:08:19.207
This isn't good. This isn't a safe place.

00:08:19.207 --> 00:08:20.791
How do you feel when you see these numbers?

00:08:21.125 --> 00:08:28.216
I'm not surprised. If you put this closer to
the ground, the number goes up to 2.

00:08:29.467 --> 00:08:35.640
Where in Namie town is the radioactivity highest?

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:41.562
Some places have such high readings, 6 or even 7
microsieverts. They're usually up in the mountains.

00:08:41.896 --> 00:08:44.482
That's where a lot of radioactivity still remains.

00:08:47.068 --> 00:08:56.494
This farm hasn't been decontaminated. This earth is the
same that was here when the nuclear power plant collapsed.

00:08:56.494 --> 00:09:04.585
The grass contaminated by the disaster still grows here.
Everyone in Namie town has lost their roots to the town.

00:09:05.253 --> 00:09:11.842
They were kicked out, left in the dust.

00:09:12.677 --> 00:09:14.262
Are there people who won't ever come back?

00:09:14.262 --> 00:09:19.600
Yes, some will never come back.
There are hardly any kids here anymore.

00:09:23.604 --> 00:09:25.523
This was once Ukedo Elementary School.

00:09:27.358 --> 00:09:28.150
On March 11,

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:29.277
2011,

00:09:29.735 --> 00:09:33.739
the school was hit with a two-story tall wave as the tsunami moved inland.

00:09:38.244 --> 00:09:41.872
Nearly a hundred students and teachers were in the school when disaster struck.

00:09:43.457 --> 00:09:44.834
all escaped with their lives.

00:09:46.335 --> 00:09:48.212
One of them was Shinichi Sato,

00:09:48.588 --> 00:09:49.422
a former teacher.

00:09:50.631 --> 00:10:00.057
That clock shows the time when the tsunami
caused the most damage in this area.

00:10:00.725 --> 00:10:06.314
Inside the school, there's a circuit that controls that clock.

00:10:06.314 --> 00:10:14.989
But when the tsunami came, it broke the circuit,
so 2.46 p.m. is when Ukedo was struck by the tsunami.

00:10:15.656 --> 00:10:18.242
How do you feel when you see that clock?

00:10:18.242 --> 00:10:26.834
By that time we had already escaped to the mountains.

00:10:26.834 --> 00:10:30.254
I'm glad we weren't here. That's why our lives were saved.

00:10:30.254 --> 00:10:34.675
But the 200 residents who were still
here then, they lost their lives to the tsunami.

00:10:34.675 --> 00:10:48.856
It was an hour after the earthquake,
but they must've not been able to escape.

00:10:50.441 --> 00:10:55.196
This area looks exactly like it did after the disaster struck.

00:10:55.196 --> 00:11:01.202
They're not going to clean it up. It's been
left as the remains of the earthguake.

00:11:02.453 --> 00:11:03.371
So they'll leave it as it is.

00:11:03.371 --> 00:11:05.456
Yes. For 10 years, it's looked like this.

00:11:05.956 --> 00:11:07.625
You can see how tremendously strong the waves must have been.

00:11:08.292 --> 00:11:12.046
If you go closer, you can see the rubble
has been swept up to the ceiling.

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:19.387
When you visit this place, I think you can understand
how strong and destructive the waves were.

00:11:20.054 --> 00:11:25.976
Do you want this school to remain here forever?

00:11:25.976 --> 00:11:29.730
This is the only building still left,

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:37.071
in the middle of all this flat land. It's where you can
really understand what happened on that day.

00:11:37.071 --> 00:11:43.244
I want this place to stay for the residents of this area.

00:11:43.994 --> 00:11:52.336
How did you manage to evacuate all 98 of your students?

00:11:52.336 --> 00:11:53.587
When the earthquake struck,

00:11:53.587 --> 00:11:59.760
we were afraid the ground under us
would open up. Everyone gathered here.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:00.094
I was still inspecting inside the school

00:12:00.261 --> 00:12:01.095
I was still inspecting inside the school

00:12:01.262 --> 00:12:02.263
was still inspecting inside the school

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:04.265
I was still inspecting inside the school

00:12:04.265 --> 00:12:06.934
so I hadn't come downstairs yet, but
everyone followed the principal's orders.

00:12:06.934 --> 00:12:12.606
You can't see it from here, but everyone
headed to Mount Ohira, a huge mountain.

00:12:13.441 --> 00:12:19.196
We left the school grounds and followed
the path to the end of the rice fields,

00:12:20.197 --> 00:12:25.369
and went up the mountain with all the children.

00:12:35.421 --> 00:12:42.303
Shinichi Sato now teaches at a newly built school that opened three years ago to a student body of just 30 children.

00:12:51.562 --> 00:12:55.900
I'm sure there weren't that many students
when you first came back to teach,

00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:59.487
but did it feel sad to be teaching in
a classroom with so few students?

00:12:59.487 --> 00:13:07.161
Before I came back, I was a part time teacher in a different
part of Fukushima. There were 30-40 students per class.

00:13:07.161 --> 00:13:14.084
It was lively and there were so many different students.
But when I came back here, there were only two desks.

00:13:14.835 --> 00:13:17.421
It was me and two students, in a big classroom.

00:13:17.421 --> 00:13:25.179
It felt sort of homey, but there wasn't any of fighting,
or conflict, that's a natural part of a child's development.

00:13:25.179 --> 00:13:35.773
Whether that's good or bad, that's difficult to say.

00:13:36.190 --> 00:13:43.197
The number of students is increasing, but it's
nowhere near what it once was. There aren't enough students.

00:13:43.197 --> 00:13:47.868
Most importantly, the kids can't ride
their bikes to go play after school.

00:13:49.036 --> 00:13:56.627
There are still wild animals roaming around, After dark,
no one is out walking. And everyone comes to school by bus.

00:13:56.627 --> 00:14:03.717
When we were kids, everyone would play in the schoolyard with
other kids. The children don't experience any of that now.

00:14:03.717 --> 00:14:16.730
Not after the disaster. I feel sorry for them,
that they'll leave childhood without those experiences.

00:14:17.398 --> 00:14:22.653
Were some residents afraid of radiation poisoning?

00:14:22.653 --> 00:14:25.739
I think at the beginning some people
were concerned, like with the water.

00:14:25.739 --> 00:14:33.330
But they've been monitoring this area closely and the
data shows it's safe, so no one's anxious anymore.

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:38.502
We drink the milk, the water, we even use the faucets.
And they inspect all the seafood that's caught.

00:14:38.502 --> 00:14:52.182
I think most people living here are more or less at peace.
They don't give radiation poisoning much thought.

00:14:52.182 --> 00:14:54.435
So is Namie town a safe place?

00:14:54.435 --> 00:15:00.941
Well, the decommissioned buildings are still here, and with
the recent earthguake, we don't know what's going to happen.

00:15:00.941 --> 00:15:10.200
I think things are getting calmer, to some extent,
but I don't think you can say it's totally safe.

00:15:12.411 --> 00:15:13.913
The disaster was a decade ago.

00:15:14.538 --> 00:15:17.541
But that doesn't mean Fukushima stopped experiencing earthquakes.

00:15:18.959 --> 00:15:20.628
Just one day before we arrived,

00:15:20.961 --> 00:15:24.256
a huge 7.3 quake shook the prefecture.

00:15:25.591 --> 00:15:27.176
It left the region without power,

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:28.427
killed one,

00:15:28.928 --> 00:15:30.220
and injured dozens more.

00:15:31.722 --> 00:15:34.058
So this is the wall of the hotel that we're staying at.

00:15:34.099 --> 00:15:34.850
And as you can see,

00:15:34.892 --> 00:15:40.940
some of the wallpaper has come and done from a 7.3 shake that happened just a few days ago.

00:15:41.565 --> 00:15:42.942
And as you go further down the hallway,

00:15:42.942 --> 00:15:43.776
it gets even worse.

00:15:44.485 --> 00:15:45.569
Here's another crack.

00:15:47.196 --> 00:15:51.784
And further down here you can see some of the plaster that's come down from the ceiling.

00:15:53.118 --> 00:15:53.285
Yeah,

00:15:53.452 --> 00:15:54.036
just right here.

00:15:55.371 --> 00:16:01.293
And the fact this happened just a few days before the 10-year passing since the triple disaster on March 11,

00:16:01.293 --> 00:16:02.294
2011,

00:16:02.753 --> 00:16:06.298
I think served as a reminder of just how tragic events were 10 years ago.

00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:09.593
Despite all of this,

00:16:10.135 --> 00:16:12.179
life is slowly returning to Namie Town.

00:16:13.222 --> 00:16:17.726
Tsukasa Yamada was drawn back home to take part in the reconstruction efforts.

00:16:19.061 --> 00:16:21.313
His daughter was born right after the disaster.

00:16:22.272 --> 00:16:24.984
He sees this as a fresh start for his family.

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:31.365
Were you concerned for your
child's health because of the radiation?

00:16:31.365 --> 00:16:39.289
Yeah, I think so. But while I'm concerned, I'm not doing
anything different to address it. We're just living our normal lives.

00:16:41.792 --> 00:16:46.797
I have heard that it might have an
effect on her health once she's an adult.

00:16:48.132 --> 00:16:52.386
So I do worry about her health as she grows up.

00:16:52.886 --> 00:16:57.141
Have you ever experienced discrimination
because you were from Fukushima?

00:16:57.474 --> 00:17:06.316
Yes, of course. People would say, "oh
you're from Fukushima? Is that OK?"

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:14.742
There were times I was treated like I was radioactive.

00:17:14.742 --> 00:17:16.243
Where dlid you experience this?

00:17:17.161 --> 00:17:17.745
Columbia

00:17:18.412 --> 00:17:21.749
The first place I evacuated to was Niigata prefecture.

00:17:22.082 --> 00:17:22.916
The first place I evacuated to was Niigata prefecture.

00:17:22.916 --> 00:17:23.417
Columbi

00:17:23.417 --> 00:17:36.180
My car still had Fukushima plates, so people would ask,
"you're from Fukushima?" It was very transparent.

00:17:36.180 --> 00:17:44.438
It's like, when one recovers from the coronavirus,
people say, "Oh, that person had covid." Jonathan Vit is this okay?

00:17:45.105 --> 00:17:53.697
So I think it's only natural that some people would pull away
when they hear Fukushima. It's a natural human reaction.

00:17:54.114 --> 00:17:59.870
Do you have hopes and dreams
for yourself, as well as Namie town?

00:18:00.788 --> 00:18:11.882
Yes, I do. Before the disaster, Namie town
had a population of about 20,000 people.

00:18:11.882 --> 00:18:19.389
I hope that through our hard work,
we can return the town to what it once was.

00:18:22.851 --> 00:18:24.561
But as the town looks to rebuild,

00:18:25.104 --> 00:18:28.273
an ominous reminder of the dangers remain in plain sight.

00:18:30.067 --> 00:18:32.736
The TEPCO-owned Daiichi Nuclear Plant,

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:35.864
which can be seen from Masami Yoshizawa's ranch.

00:18:37.241 --> 00:18:39.159
This is it. I see it.

00:18:40.828 --> 00:18:42.162
How many kilometers away is the plant?

00:18:42.162 --> 00:18:43.664
In a straight line, 14 kilometers.

00:18:48.168 --> 00:18:58.428
On March 17th, I went to the second floor and used binoculars
to see how the SDF was cleaning up the Daiichi nuclear plant.

00:18:58.428 --> 00:19:06.687
I could see the flames. I thought, "the Self
Defense Force, the firefighters, they're all going to die."

00:19:07.521 --> 00:19:16.363
The TEPCO employees decided to retreat, so they
escaped to the second power plant, fleeing the first one.

00:19:17.114 --> 00:19:26.290
I had been here a week and when I saw that, I decided
that those bastards at TEPCO, I'm never going to forgive them.

00:19:26.874 --> 00:19:34.464
When the SDF were working hard to protect us, TEPCO
ran away. I don't care, I'll spend the rest of my life fighting this plant.

00:19:34.464 --> 00:19:47.144
I will remain against nuclear power. Until the day I die, I will
take care of these cows and fight for a world without nuclear power.

00:19:47.895 --> 00:19:51.899
We can't sell our rice, our vegetables. We can't return home.

00:19:52.649 --> 00:20:02.159
Our town has become Cherynobyl. Everyone, the
Fukushima nuclear disaster could be Tokyo's tomorrow.

00:20:02.743 --> 00:20:07.998
400 Wagyu cow herders had to leave their cows and escape.

00:20:08.248 --> 00:20:17.007
Keep fighting! For our future, for our
children, for ourselves, keep fighting!

00:20:25.349 --> 00:20:30.354
The farmers who let their cows starve to
death don't want to see the ones that are still alive.

00:20:31.772 --> 00:20:37.110
Because when they do, they're reminded of the past.

00:20:38.946 --> 00:20:48.455
But I don't think they made a mistake. It was
unavoidable. At the time, they had no other choice.

00:20:48.455 --> 00:20:53.877
They couldn't care for their cows.
So they apologized instead.

00:20:54.628 --> 00:21:04.805
They said, "I'm sorry. To save us humans,
you have to stay here and rot / die. I'm so sorry.

00:21:06.139 --> 00:21:16.066
That's why they can't have cows again. But they weren't wrong.
And the ones who listened and killed their herds aren't wrong either.

00:21:17.567 --> 00:21:25.993
I'm not wrong either. There's no right
answer here. There are multiple answers.

00:21:25.993 --> 00:21:32.582
I understand that now, 10 years later. We can't
be emotional and argue about this anymore.

00:21:33.333 --> 00:21:39.673
We have to accept each other, understand each other.
That's what's happened, over these 10 years.

00:21:53.186 --> 00:21:53.854
This is Futaba,

00:21:54.354 --> 00:21:57.524
a town only 4 kilometers from the nuclear power plant,

00:21:58.066 --> 00:21:59.860
where time seems to stand still.

00:22:10.829 --> 00:22:12.164
This is the main street here.

00:22:13.665 --> 00:22:19.212
Futaba is still inside what Japanese officials declared a difficult-to-return zone.

00:22:20.005 --> 00:22:23.467
meaning radiation levels are too high to allow people to move back.

00:22:27.721 --> 00:22:29.139
There's a term in Japanese,

00:22:29.723 --> 00:22:30.265
shouganai,

00:22:31.266 --> 00:22:34.144
that is often used to acknowledge conflict or pain.

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:39.232
The Fukushima residents we spoke to paid respect to their past in this way.

00:22:43.612 --> 00:22:45.197
As you walk through the town of Futaba,

00:22:45.697 --> 00:22:48.784
you can still see the remains of the triple disaster ten years ago.

00:22:50.243 --> 00:22:51.912
The buildings haven't been replaced yet,

00:22:52.245 --> 00:22:56.291
and the destruction serves as a reminder of the tragedies this town faced.

00:22:57.209 --> 00:22:59.419
Even as some residents hope for a brighter future,

00:22:59.711 --> 00:23:01.421
they live with the past because it's

00:23:02.756 --> 00:23:03.215
Shogunai.

